WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.937
Picture the rail above a café kitchen.

2
00:00:04.937 --> 00:00:09.647
The cashier clips each order slip to it, and the cooks take them from the front.

3
00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:11.191
A cook takes a slip.

4
00:00:11.191 --> 00:00:12.868
When can it go in the bin?

5
00:00:12.868 --> 00:00:15.772
When she takes it, or when the dish goes out?

6
00:00:15.772 --> 00:00:17.966
And what if she walks out halfway?

7
00:00:17.966 --> 00:00:19.128
Hold that thought.

8
00:00:19.367 --> 00:00:20.775
The kitchen has a rule.

9
00:00:20.775 --> 00:00:25.182
The slip stays clipped above the cook's station until the dish goes out.

10
00:00:25.182 --> 00:00:26.896
Only then is it thrown away.

11
00:00:27.167 --> 00:00:32.784
If the cook walks out halfway, her slip goes back on the rail, and the next free cook picks it up.

12
00:00:33.133 --> 00:00:35.768
Programs pass messages the same way.

13
00:00:35.768 --> 00:00:41.403
A message is a small piece of data, such as: an account asks to pay 40 euros.

14
00:00:41.667 --> 00:00:45.312
A broker is a program in the middle that holds messages.

15
00:00:45.312 --> 00:00:46.744
Its queue is the rail.

16
00:00:46.744 --> 00:00:52.993
The program that sends is the cashier, and each program that reads is a cook, called a consumer.

17
00:00:53.233 --> 00:00:57.474
We'll use RabbitMQ, a free broker program that many companies run.

18
00:00:57.474 --> 00:01:02.228
Our consumer is the ledger, the program that keeps each account's balance.

19
00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:07.648
Now, the tempting shortcut: delete each message the moment the broker sends it.

20
00:01:07.933 --> 00:01:10.912
Watch the ledger take m1 and crash halfway.

21
00:01:10.912 --> 00:01:12.989
The broker already deleted it.

22
00:01:12.989 --> 00:01:14.375
The payment is gone.

23
00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:18.870
That's a kitchen that bins each slip as soon as a cook glances at it.

24
00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:19.489
Very tidy.

25
00:01:19.489 --> 00:01:22.769
Until the cook goes home with your lunch in her head.

26
00:01:23.033 --> 00:01:27.434
RabbitMQ calls this auto-ack, and its own guide calls it unsafe.

27
00:01:27.434 --> 00:01:31.559
Nothing twice, and some never: that's at-most-once delivery.

28
00:01:31.833 --> 00:01:34.994
Let's put m1 back, and do it the kitchen's way.

29
00:01:34.994 --> 00:01:37.280
The broker sends m1, and keeps it.

30
00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:43.399
Until the consumer is done, m1 is unacked: still in the queue, set aside for that consumer.

31
00:01:43.399 --> 00:01:45.215
The slip above the station.

32
00:01:45.467 --> 00:01:50.305
After it books the payment, the consumer sends a short note: done, delete it.

33
00:01:50.305 --> 00:01:52.819
That note is an acknowledgement, or ack.

34
00:01:53.033 --> 00:02:00.551
If the connection closes before the ack, the broker puts the message back and sends it again, flagged as redelivered.

35
00:02:00.551 --> 00:02:01.708
That's redelivery.

36
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:03.097
Here's a puzzle.

37
00:02:03.097 --> 00:02:08.996
The ledger books m2, adding it to the balance, and crashes just before its ack leaves.

38
00:02:08.996 --> 00:02:10.231
What happens next?

39
00:02:10.231 --> 00:02:11.328
Pause and guess.

40
00:02:14.533 --> 00:02:18.591
The broker can't tell this crash from one before the work started.

41
00:02:18.591 --> 00:02:22.035
So m2 comes back, and the ledger books it a second time.

42
00:02:23.933 --> 00:02:29.359
Nothing is lost, and some messages are handled twice: that's at-least-once delivery.

43
00:02:29.359 --> 00:02:34.656
The ledger must spot the repeat itself, and the lesson's worked example shows how.

44
00:02:34.933 --> 00:02:36.213
One ledger is slow.

45
00:02:36.213 --> 00:02:42.345
So we run two copies, A and B, on one queue, and the broker gives each message to only one.

46
00:02:42.345 --> 00:02:44.703
They're called competing consumers.

47
00:02:44.933 --> 00:02:47.695
Five messages for account 17 wait in order.

48
00:02:47.695 --> 00:02:49.494
Each card payment takes two.

49
00:02:49.494 --> 00:02:54.439
When you tap your card, the bank first sets the money aside: that's the auth.

50
00:02:54.439 --> 00:02:58.357
Later the money really moves to the shop: that's the capture.

51
00:02:58.633 --> 00:03:02.143
The ledger must book each auth before its capture.

52
00:03:02.143 --> 00:03:05.021
And this is a queue: first in, first out.

53
00:03:05.021 --> 00:03:06.495
So the order is safe.

54
00:03:06.495 --> 00:03:06.916
Right?

55
00:03:07.167 --> 00:03:09.924
Each holds one message at a time.

56
00:03:09.924 --> 00:03:12.096
m1 goes to A, and m2 to B.

57
00:03:12.096 --> 00:03:13.684
Both book, and ack.

58
00:03:13.684 --> 00:03:14.937
So far so good.

59
00:03:15.467 --> 00:03:20.785
Next, B takes m3, the auth of 25, and A takes m4, its capture.

60
00:03:21.067 --> 00:03:22.900
Now B crashes before its ack.

61
00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:27.008
The broker puts m3 back at its old place, the front of the queue.

62
00:03:27.008 --> 00:03:29.789
In what order does the ledger book the rest?

63
00:03:29.789 --> 00:03:30.800
Pause and guess.

64
00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:36.329
Consumer A was still busy with m4.

65
00:03:36.329 --> 00:03:42.013
It finishes, and books the capture of 25, for money the ledger never saw set aside.

66
00:03:42.267 --> 00:03:46.970
Then A gets m3, marked redelivered, and books it.

67
00:03:46.970 --> 00:03:47.738
Then m5.

68
00:03:47.738 --> 00:03:51.481
The ledger's order: m1, m2, m4, m3, m5.

69
00:03:51.733 --> 00:03:52.236
Well...

70
00:03:52.236 --> 00:03:54.605
that's a capture before its auth.

71
00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:58.108
Nothing was lost, and nothing was booked twice.

72
00:03:58.108 --> 00:04:01.819
The queue even kept its order: it handed out m3 before m4.

73
00:04:01.819 --> 00:04:05.851
The work didn't, because two machines finish at their own pace.

74
00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:12.005
A queue is first in, first out, FIFO for short, only at the moment it hands messages out.

75
00:04:12.005 --> 00:04:14.924
It can't make two consumers finish in order.

76
00:04:15.167 --> 00:04:17.374
Here the café picture stops working.

77
00:04:17.374 --> 00:04:21.238
A broker writes each message to disk on several machines first.

78
00:04:21.238 --> 00:04:25.469
And it never knows if a dish was cooked, only whether a cook said so.

79
00:04:25.733 --> 00:04:28.390
Here's that consumer in Java and Kotlin.

80
00:04:28.390 --> 00:04:33.704
false turns auto-ack off, the middle books the payment, and basicAck comes last.

81
00:04:34.167 --> 00:04:41.171
The fix for order, inside a broker, is one consumer per queue, which RabbitMQ calls single active consumer.

82
00:04:41.171 --> 00:04:44.378
You keep the order, and give up sharing the work.

83
00:04:44.378 --> 00:04:47.520
The next two lessons show a log that keeps both.

84
00:04:47.733 --> 00:04:50.229
Ever had the same receipt email twice?

85
00:04:50.229 --> 00:04:54.104
Now you know: somewhere, a consumer crashed before its ack.

86
00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:58.171
So: the slip stays above the station until the dish goes out.

87
00:04:58.171 --> 00:04:59.593
A crash brings it back.

88
00:04:59.593 --> 00:05:02.313
And two cooks can finish in the wrong order.

89
00:05:02.767 --> 00:05:03.923
One quick check.

90
00:05:03.923 --> 00:05:07.971
Turn auto-ack back on, and let B crash holding m3 again.

91
00:05:07.971 --> 00:05:09.850
What does the ledger book?

92
00:05:09.850 --> 00:05:11.007
Pause and think.

93
00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:15.470
m1, m2, m4, m5.

94
00:05:15.470 --> 00:05:23.771
m3 was deleted when it was sent, so it never comes back, and the capture of 25 has no auth at all.
